  "blocks": [],
  "meta": {
    "title": "Lengths divisible by 2 or by 3",
    "blurb": "aⁿ where 2 | n or 3 | n. Union by ε-moves: the start state jumps silently into a 2-cycle and a 3-cycle, and the word is accepted if either cycle ends at home. The DFA for the same language is a single 6-cycle.",
    "inputs": [
      {
        "w": "",
        "expect": "accept",
        "label": "n = 0"
      },
      {
        "w": "aa",
        "expect": "accept",
        "label": "2"
      },
      {
        "w": "aaa",
        "expect": "accept",
        "label": "3"
      },
      {
        "w": "aaaa",
        "expect": "accept",
        "label": "4"
      },
      {
        "w": "aaaaaa",
        "expect": "accept",
        "label": "6"
      },
      {
        "w": "a",
        "expect": "reject",
        "label": "1"
      },
      {
        "w": "aaaaa",
        "expect": "reject",
        "label": "5"
      },
      {
        "w": "aaaaaaa",
        "expect": "reject",
        "label": "7"
      }
    ],
    "library": {
      "author": {
        "login": "thethinkmachine"
      },
      "license": "CC-BY-4.0",
      "tags": [
        "union",
        "unary",
        "epsilon-moves"
      ],
      "difficulty": "intro",
      "readme": "The standard proof that regular languages are closed under union, drawn: put the two machines side by side and add a new start with an ε-move to each.\n\nDeterministically, the machine has to track *n* mod 2 and *n* mod 3 at once, which is *n* mod 6 by the Chinese remainder theorem — a cycle of six states, of which the remainders 0, 2, 3 and 4 accept. Unary languages like this one are where the size gap between NFAs and DFAs is governed by least common multiples."
    }
  }
}
